# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from nums import *

H1("5. Conclusion")
P("The layered tumour and normal tissue model of Jangid, Singh and Mukhopadhyay [13] was extended to a fractional-order modified Green–Lindsay bioheat thermoelasticity with a temperature-dependent perfusion law and an optional temperature-dependent conductivity. The fractional terms are the Caputo derivatives of order $1+\\alpha$ of temperature and dilatation in the energy equation; the strain-rate lag, the perfusion sink and the source are integer-order. "
  "The model was solved by a Galerkin finite element method with linear elements, a BDF2 convolution quadrature for all time derivatives and a Picard iteration for the nonlinear terms, and a Legendre wavelet collocation method was not implemented. An exact Laplace-domain solution of the linear layered problem, in which the Caputo term becomes $s^{1+\\alpha}$, supplied the validation.")
P("The main findings are the following. The code reproduces the Laplace-domain solution to " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.4"]["th"]) + " per cent in temperature and about " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.4"]["u"]) + " per cent in displacement for $\\alpha=0.6$ at $t=0.4$, converges at second order in time for smooth data and at first order for the step surface temperature, and is accurate to several per cent for the integer-order case where jump fronts limit it. "
  "The nonlinear terms were verified by a steady-state comparison with an independent code to " + sct(STEADY[0][1], 1) + ", and the Picard iteration converged superlinearly with a mean of about " + f2(sum(R["picard"]["iters"]) / len(R["picard"]["iters"])) + " iterations per step. "
  "In the source scaling the perfusion and laser numbers are about $10^{-13}$ and $10^{-12}$, so that they have no effect, and the MGT model coincides with the LS-type model for the same reason.")
P("Decreasing the fractional order removes the sharp thermal front and lets a small temperature precursor reach distant points earlier; it lowers the tumour-edge stress peak from " + f2(edge_peak("base")[0]) + " at $\\alpha=1$ to " + f2(edge_peak("alpha_0.3")[0]) + " at $\\alpha=0.3$. The strain-rate lag leaves the temperature unchanged and raises the early edge stress, while a perfusion law that shuts down above a critical temperature reduces the cooling effect of perfusion at the hottest part of the tumour from " + pct(1 - snapth("Pi_10_c0", 0.6, 0.2) / snapth("Pi_0", 0.6, 0.2)) + " per cent (constant perfusion, $\\Pi=10$) to " + pct(1 - snapth("Pi_10", 0.6, 0.2) / snapth("Pi_0", 0.6, 0.2)) + " per cent. "
  "The fields do not vanish beyond the interface once the thermal front has arrived, which in the present parameters happens at $t=" + f3(th_arr) + "$. These are properties of an illustrative model in dimensionless form.")
P("Future work should implement the wavelet collocation variant and compare it with the present validated solution, should replace the illustrative relaxation times and perfusion parameters with values taken from measurements with verified sources, and should treat the length and time scales of a real treatment instead of the nanometre-scale dimensionless setup. The reference list must also be completed to the full set of recent publications.")

